# ffbox secrets. `sh ffbox/setup.sh` installs this template at ~/.config/ffbox/secrets.env with
# mode 600 and never overwrites it afterwards; fill in the empty values below and you are done.
#
# This file stays on the host. Docker reads it at run time and injects the values as environment
# variables; nothing here is ever baked into the image (image layers are readable by anyone who
# can run `docker history`, and a baked secret survives in the layer cache forever).
#
# Do NOT pass these as command-line arguments instead — argv is world-readable through
# /proc/<pid>/cmdline for the life of the process, and it lands in your shell history.

# --- Claude ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ONE TOKEN PER CLAUDE ACCOUNT, NUMBERED FROM 1. Each is a long-lived subscription token;
# generate one on the host, signed in as that account, with:
#   claude setup-token
# They bill against the subscription, no API key involved, and unlike mounting
# ~/.claude/.credentials.json they can't be rewritten underneath you by a refresh.
#
# ONLY THE FIRST NON-EMPTY SLOT IS SPENT. ffbox hands that one to every container it starts and
# ffwatch classifies with it; nothing reads the others. They are here so that ffweb's /claude
# page can report each account's five-hour and weekly usage — the question on a busy box is not
# "am I out" but "which account still has room", and that cannot be asked about an account
# whose token nobody wrote down. Moving the box onto another account is then reordering two
# lines in this file and a restart, rather than an OAuth flow at the worst possible moment.
#
# A GAP IS NOT THE END OF THE LIST: slot 2 filled with slot 1 blank is a revoked first key, and
# everything here keeps scanning to slot 16.
#
# A note on what the /claude page can see. `claude setup-token` mints a token WITHOUT the
# `user:profile` scope, so Anthropic's usage document refuses it and the page falls back to
# reading the five-hour and weekly windows off a one-token API call. That works on every token
# here, and costs a few tokens per key every twenty minutes. What it cannot show is the account
# name or the per-model weekly caps -- those need a token minted by an interactive login.
CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN1=
#CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN2=
#CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN3=

# The OLD, UNNUMBERED SPELLING. Still read, and used as a pool of one when no numbered slot is
# set, so a secrets.env written before the pool existed keeps working with no edit. Prefer the
# numbered names on anything new: an unnumbered token shows on the /claude page as a legacy
# entry rather than as slot 1 of a pool somebody can add to.
#CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=

# --- Unity (HOST SIDE ONLY) ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# These are read by the HOST, to mint and refresh the licence file. They are NEVER passed into a
# container -- not an agent run, not a pool worker, not a CI job. That distinction is the whole of
# this change, and it is the same posture GH_PR_TOKEN and the Discord token below already have.
#
# WHY THEY ARE STILL HERE. Until 2026-09-01 ffbox handed these to every container, which activated
# online on start. That put a full Unity account credential -- the same identity as the Asset Store
# account and the org membership -- inside a container running
# `claude -p --dangerously-skip-permissions` over text strangers wrote, readable out of
# /proc/self/environ by anything in it.
#
# The container now gets a .ulf FILE instead, mounted read-only. But a Personal .ulf carries a
# rolling ~24-hour UpdateDate rather than an annual expiry (measured: no StopDate at all), so
# something has to renew it -- and that something is the host, right before it creates a container:
#
#     ffbox/unity-offline-license.sh ensure 4
#
# runs at every container launch, does nothing but read a date when the licence is healthy, and
# re-activates when under four hours remain. Leave these blank and the licence cannot renew itself;
# `mint` will prompt for them interactively instead.
#
# AN ACCESS TOKEN IS BETTER THAN THE PASSWORD where you can get one: it expires, it is revocable
# without a password reset, and it is the only way past 2FA on the account.
#UNITY_ACCESS_TOKEN=
UNITY_EMAIL=
UNITY_PASSWORD=

# NOT NEEDED, AND NOT USED. UNITY_SERIAL and UNITY_LICENSE_FILE belonged to the old online serial
# activation; nothing reads them now. A Personal licence has no serial to give.

# --- ffweb (the page) ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# ffweb serves over https behind a login. The accounts are hardcoded in ffweb.py — Ben and
# Lothsahn, sharing a passphrase — and these override without a patch, which is the point of
# them being here: a machine on a LAN address should not run the password that is in git.
#
# FFWEB_PASSWORD sets the password for every account. FFWEB_USER narrows the table to that one
# name (matched case-insensitively) with that password, and drops the rest.
#FFWEB_USER=
#FFWEB_PASSWORD=
#
# The certificate is self-signed and generated into ~/ffbox-state/tls on first start, so the
# browser warns once and that warning is correct. Point --tls-cert/--tls-key at a real pair if
# you have one; ffweb never overwrites a certificate it finds.

# --- Discord + GitHub (HOST SIDE ONLY) -----------------------------------------------------
# Neither of these is ever passed into a container, and that is load-bearing rather than
# tidiness.
#
# FFDISCORD_APP_TOKEN is the bot token (FFDISCORD_TOKEN is the older name and still works).
# The host does every Discord read and every Discord write:
# ffwatch pulls messages and attachments before a run and posts the reply after it, so the
# agent — which is reading text written by strangers — never holds a credential that can speak
# as the bot. ffdiscord and ffdiscord-listener both read this, or the same value from the
# "discord.app_token" key in ~/.config/ffbox/config.json.
#FFDISCORD_APP_TOKEN=
#
# GH_PR_TOKEN is what opens pull requests, and opening them is ALL it may do. "Nothing merges,
# ever" is held by this token never entering the container, NOT by the agent's deny list — a deny
# pattern like Bash(git push*) is a tripwire that `sh -c 'git push'` walks straight through. The
# container has no push credential and no gh binary, so publication is physically the host's job.
#
# A FINE-GRAINED token: resource owner Final-Factory, repository access Final-Factory/FinalFactory
# and nothing else, PULL REQUESTS at read and write, CONTENTS AT READ, every other permission at No
# access. Metadata read is added for you.
#
# CONTENTS READ IS NOT OPTIONAL, and this file said "every other permission at No access" until
# 2026-09-02, which is why a box ran for weeks unable to open a single pull request. POST /pulls
# reads the head and base refs before it creates anything; without contents:read it answers
# 422 "not all refs are readable" while every GET the client makes keeps working, so the token
# looks healthy right up to the moment it has to open something.
#
# Do NOT give it contents:WRITE, whatever an older copy of this file said: merging a pull request
# needs contents:write alongside pull-requests:write, so a token carrying both is a token that can
# merge, and this one is deliberately not. Read cannot move a branch or merge anything, so the
# line is in the same place it always was. Pushing the work branch uses a
# SEPARATE credential -- the one git finds in ~/.git-credentials -- which is never spliced into a
# push url, because argv is world-readable through /proc. ffbox/CREDENTIALS.md has the whole
# split, and a three-line probe for checking a token before you trust it.
#
# ffwatch reads it from the ENVIRONMENT rather than from ~/.config/ffbox/config.json, so it never
# sits on disk beside channel ids. GH_TOKEN is still read, as the name this had before 2026-09-02.
#GH_PR_TOKEN=
#
# ONE PR TOKEN PER POOL, OPTIONAL. A pool that names a key here publishes with it instead of the
# box-wide GH_PR_TOKEN above, so ffagent -- the lane that runs text written by strangers in the
# forum -- and ffdev need not share a credential. The NAME goes in config.json under
# pools.<class>.github.pr_token; the token stays in this file. Both want the same fine-grained
# permissions as GH_PR_TOKEN: pull requests read and write, contents READ, nothing else.
#
# Mint them from two different GitHub accounts if you want the split to be visible: same code
# either way, but then every branch and pull request says which lane proposed it, and branch
# protection and CODEOWNERS can treat the two differently. One account gives you rotation and
# revocation on separate schedules and nothing more.
#
# NAMING A KEY YOU HAVE NOT SET STOPS THAT POOL PUBLISHING, on purpose. There is no fallback to
# GH_PR_TOKEN: falling back would hand ffagent the dev lane's credential at the exact moment
# somebody believed the two were separated. The run's work is still pushed, the reply names the
# missing key, and the reconcile sweep opens the pull request once it is here -- no restart.
# Leave both unset and unnamed to keep one token for the box, which is the default.
#GH_PR_TOKEN_FFAGENT=
#GH_PR_TOKEN_FFDEV=
